2026-06-30 15:50:48,488 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-cd30b294-940d-48dd-9887-5bc5acd56329', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the crosslinking reagent and reaction chemistry.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cross-linker\nsdrf_column: comment[cross-linker]\nontology: XLMOD (e.g. XLMOD:00004 for DSS/BS3)\ndescription: The chemical crosslinking reagent used. This is the primary crosslinker\n  identifier and must be formatted as a structured key-value string.\noutput_format: NT=<name>;AC=<XLMOD or ChEBI accession>;CL=<yes/no>;TA=<residue targets>\n  where:\n    NT = full chemical name or common abbreviation (e.g. BS3, DSSO, DSS, DSBU, EDC,\n         formaldehyde, SDA, sulfo-SDA, BMOE, Sulfo-LC-SDA, PIR, CBDPS)\n    AC = XLMOD or ChEBI accession if identifiable (else null)\n    CL = yes if MS-cleavable (DSSO, DSBU, DSBSO, DHSO, BMSO, SDASO, SSD, CBDPS, iQlnk, etc.), no if non-cleavable (BS3, DSS, EDC, PhoX, SDA, PIR, etc.), null if not stated\n    TA = comma-separated reactive residues (e.g. K,nterm or K,S,T,Y or D,E or C)\ninstructions: Extract from Methods section. If multiple crosslinkers, return a list.\n  ALWAYS populate NT with the reagent name as written in the text, even if the XLMOD\n  accession is unknown — set AC=null in that case. Only return value=null if no\n  crosslinker is mentioned anywhere in the provided text.\n  Common cleavable: DSSO (K,S,T,Y; AC=XLMOD:02126), DSBU (K,S,T,Y; AC=XLMOD:02120),\n    DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=null), Azide-A-DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02222), Alkyne-A-DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02223),\n    DSAU (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nDirected evolution of the multicopper oxidase laccase for cell surface proximity labeling and electron microscopy\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nEnzymes that oxidize aromatic substrates have shown utility in a range of cell-based technologies including live cell proximity labeling (PL) and electron microscopy (EM), but are associated with drawbacks such as the need for toxic H2O2. Here, we explore laccases as a novel enzyme class for PL and EM in mammalian cells. LaccID, generated via 11 rounds of directed evolution from an ancestral fungal laccase, catalyzes the one-electron oxidation of diverse aromatic substrates using O2 instead of toxic H2O2, and exhibits activity selective to the surface plasma membrane of both living and fixed cells. We show that LaccID can be used with mass spectrometry-based proteomics to map the changing surface composition of T cells that engage with tumor cells via antigen-specific T cell receptors. In addition, we use LaccID as a genetically-encodable tag for EM visualization of cell surface features in mammalian cell culture and in the fly brain. Our study paves the way for future cell-based applications of LaccID.\n\n[METHODS]\nOn-bead trypsin digestion of biotinylated proteins Biotinylated proteins bound to streptavidin magnetic beads were subjected to four washes with 200 uL of 50 mM Tris-HCl (pH=7.5) buffer. After removing the final wash, the beads were incubated twice at room temperature (RT) in 80uL of the digestion buffer - 2M Urea, 50nM Tris-HCl, 1mM DTT, and 0.4ug trypsin – while shaking at 1000 rpm. The first incubation lasted 1 hour, followed by the second incubation of 30 minutes. After each incubation, the supernatant was collected and transferred into a separate tube. The beads were then washed twice with 60 uL of 2M Urea/50mM Tris-HCl buffer. The resulting washes were combined with the digestion supernatant. The pooled eluate of each sample was then spun down at 5000 x g for 30 sec to collect the supernatant. The samples were subsequently reduced with 4 mM DTT for 30 minutes at RT with shaking at 1000 rpm, followed by alkylation with 10 mM Iodoacetamide for 45 min in the dark at RT while shaking at 1000 rpm. Overnight digestion of the samples was performed by adding 0.5ug of trypsin to each sample. The following morning, the samples were acidified with neat formic acid (FA) to the final concentration of 1% FA (pH<3).   Digested peptide samples were desalted using in-house packed C18 (3M) StageTips. C18 StageTips were conditioned sequentially with 100 uL of 100% methanol (MeOH), 100 uL of 50% (vol/vol) acetonitrile (MeCN) with 0.1% (vol/vol) FA, and two washes of 100uL of 0.1% (vol/vol) FA. Acidified peptides were loaded onto the C18 StageTips and washed twice with 100 uL of 0.1% FA. The peptides were then eluted from the C18 resin using 50 uL of 50% MeCN/ 0.1% FA. The desalted peptide samples were snap-frozen and vacuum-centrifuged until completely dry. TMT labeling and fractionation Desalted peptides were labeled with TMTpro reagents (Thermo Fisher Scientific). Each peptide sample was resuspended in 80 μL of 50 mM HEPES and labeled with 20 uL of the 25 ug/uL TMT reagents in MeCN. The samples were then incubated at RT for 1 hour while shaking at 1000 rpm. To quench the TMT-labeling reaction, 4 μL of 5% hydroxylamine was added to each sample, followed by a 15-minute incubation at RT with shaking. TMT-labeled samples were combined and vacuum-centrifuged to dry. The samples were then reconstituted in 200 μL of 0.1% FA and desalted on a C18 StageTips using the previously described protocol. The desalted TMT-labeled combined sample was then dried to completion.  The combined TMT-labeled peptide sample was fractionated by basic reverse phase (bRP) fractionation using an in-house packed SDB-RPS (3M) StageTip. The SDB-RPS StageTip was first conditioned sequentially with 100 μL of 100% MeOH, 100 μL 50% MeCN/0.1% FA, and two washes of 100 μL 0.1% FA. The peptide sample was reconstituted in 0.1% FA and loaded onto the SDB-RPS StageTip. Peptides were then eluted from the StageTip in eight fractions using 20 mM ammonium formate buffers with increasing (vol/vol) concentrations of MeCN (5%, 7.5%, 10%, 12.5%, 15%, 20%, 25%, and 45%). The eight fractions were then dried down to completion.   Liquid chromatography and mass spectrometry All peptide samples were separated and analyzed on an online liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (LC-MS/MS) system, consisting of a Vanquish Neo UPHLC (Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled to an Orbitrap Exploris 480 (Thermo Fisher Scientific). All peptide fractions were reconstituted in 9 uL of 3% MeCN/ 0.1% FA. 4 uL of each fraction was injected onto a microcapillary column (Picofrit with 10 µm tip opening / 75 µm diameter, New Objective, PF360-75-10-N-5), packed in-house with 30 cm of C18 silica material (1.5 µm ReproSil-Pur C18-AQ medium, Dr. Maisch GmbH, r119.aq) and heated to 50 °C using column heater sleeves (PhoenixST). Peptides were eluted into the Orbitrap Exploris 480 at a flow rate of 200 nL/min. The bRP fractions were run on a 110min-method, including a linear 84 min gradient from 94.6% solvent A (0.1% formic acid) to 27% solvent B (99.9% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid), followed by a linear 9 min gradient from 27% solvent B to 54% solvent B.   Mass spectrometry was conducted using a data-dependent acquisition mode, where MS1 spectra were measured with a resolution of 60,000, a normalized AGC target of 300%, and a mass range from 350 to 1800 m/z. MS2 spectra were acquired for the top 20 most abundant ions per cycle at a resolution of 45,000, an AGC target of 30%, an isolation window of 0.7 m/z and a normalized collision energy of 34. The dynamic exclusion time was set to 20 s, and the peptide match and isotope exclusion functions were enabled.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
